Expose a read-only, identity-bound membership view of the worker registry at
GET /observer/endpoints, for external confirmation of who would actually
answer, with the gateway's own control-plane operational state included.

THE PURPOSE. The gateway's existing surfaces answer a different question than
the one external confirmation asks. /worker_list reports the registry; the
request path additionally applies health and circuit-breaker filters on the way
to each request, so a registered worker the selection would never pick is
invisible in a listing and yet occupies a place in the pool's bookkeeping.
Confirmation needs the selection-time view: per worker, the same predicates the
dispatcher evaluates, evaluated by the same accessor calls -- is_healthy() and
circuit_breaker().can_execute(), whose conjunction is_available() is exactly
what the request path consults (src/core/worker.rs:124-129).

WHAT THE GATEWAY VIEW ADDS. This gateway routes every discovery-side mutation
through a per-URL lane queue (added by
0001-discovery-readiness-and-deselect-removal.patch), and the queue's own
bookkeeping is part of what an external confirmation needs to see: whether a
URL's lane is busy or has jobs waiting, and what the last control-plane job for
that URL recorded (including a failure, which the queue keeps until the
operation succeeds). The view reads those through the queue's public getters --
a new read-only lane_occupancy() accessor on JobQueue and the existing
get_status() -- so the payload shows the machinery without being able to move
it. A gateway configured without the queue omits these fields.

READ-ONLY, AND WHY THAT IS THE WHOLE CONTRACT. The handler contains no policy
and no mutation: it iterates the registry's public snapshot
(WorkerRegistry::get_all_with_ids), reads is_healthy(),
circuit_breaker().state() and can_execute() off each live worker, and
serializes. The lane read iterates a DashMap snapshot; get_status clones an
existing entry. No fault-injection, no force-failure, no configuration of any
kind: there is no input this endpoint accepts that changes what the gateway
does, which is exactly the boundary the fault-injection ruling drew and the
reason cells that need a workflow to fail stay outside what any runtime can
drive.

IDENTITY BOUNDARY. The registry is URL-keyed and the gateway does not know
Kubernetes Pod identities beyond the UID strings discovery already carries, so
the payload carries the URL, its port, the role name
(regular/prefill/decode), and the URL's host as `pod_hint` -- a convenience
echo, never an authoritative identity. Binding an endpoint to a Pod UID is
performed by the operator at collection time from its own Kubernetes state.

FRESHNESS. `boot_generation` is the process start time in milliseconds:
constant within a process, different after every restart, so a consumer can
tell a restarted gateway from a converging one. `now_ms` is the response time.
`registry_revision` is a process-local counter that increments whenever the
serialized worker array changes (deterministic URL ordering makes the array
comparable), so equal consecutive revisions mean the registry content is
unchanged between them.

PLACEMENT. The handler is a new module (sgl-model-gateway/src/observer.rs)
wired with one mod declaration and one route line in the admin route group --
behind the same control-plane auth every admin route already carries -- plus
one read-only accessor on JobQueue. No existing handler, middleware, or policy
file is modified.

Written against sgl-project/sglang gateway-v0.3.1 (sgl-model-gateway) on top of
0001-discovery-readiness-and-deselect-removal.patch.
